      "id": 1,
      "text": "Kernersville began in 1756 when Irishman Caleb Story received a 400‑acre land grant from the Royal Colony of Carolina.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kernersvillemuseum.org/resources",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1817 Joseph Kerner bought the tavern, and the town later adopted the name Kernersville after his family.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kernersville%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The arrival of the railroad in the early 1870s prompted Kernersville’s incorporation that same year.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kernersville%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Today the 1873 Train Depot, recently restored, lets visitors glimpse how tobacco, textile and furniture goods once moved through town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kernersvillenc.com/landmarks-and-places-to-see/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Körner’s Folly, an architectural marvel, houses artist Jule Gilmer and showcases her design legacy.",
